{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head 79138ad22fd88a52eb7007b5575fe996c8228472, on one narrow, two-line defect. Everything I blocked on last round is honestly closed and verified by execution. Artifacts at this head: f8dac26f (internal/kernel, named), d2a19473 (internal/app), bde31ece (docs).\n\nGATE, the thing that blocked last time: GREEN. At the exact head, serialized behind /tmp/gitseq-docset.lock with no other go test running, go vet ./... rc=0, go build ./... rc=0, go test ./... -count=1 rc=0, every package ok, docset included at 115s. The three deterministic failures I reported are gone: cmd/gitseq-mcp ok 61.7s, internal/app ok 58.7s, internal/service ok 26.8s.\n\nMY FOUR EARLIER FINDINGS, EACH CHECKED.\nBlocker, selector clearing: CLOSED, and closed the right way. The disclosure assertions were repaired, not deleted - all three tests still assert the cold-audit, corrupt-checkpoint and profile-mismatch paths, and now reach them through the new Workspace.InvalidateCheckpoint, which removes the pointer file and rewinds the ref to genesis. The lever is real and reachable: cmd/gs checkpoint-clear, TestCheckpointClearRemovesBothPersistentSelectors proves both selectors go and the next read is SnapshotSourceColdFullAudit, and GITSEQ_CHECKPOINT=off is proved to force a cold audit while changing neither selector. Documented in a new reference page whose example the docset gate executes.\nArchitecture: CLOSED. grep for os.Open/OpenFile/Create/CreateTemp/MkdirAll/Mkdir/Rename/WriteFile/ReadFile/Remove/Stat/Lstat across internal/kernel non-test files returns nothing. The I/O moved behind a two-method CheckpointPointer interface and lives in internal/app/checkpoint.go, and the architecture.md row now states exactly that, including \"It performs no local checkpoint filesystem I/O\". The page matches reality.\nSecurity note 1, genesis into a filesystem path: CLOSED. validateGenesis is wired into both OpenObserved and AttachConfig, before any path is built, with a test that rejects a path-shaped genesis on both routes and asserts no local state is created.\nSecurity note 2, ref hostage to the pointer write: CLOSED, and inverted correctly. writeCheckpoint now updates the ref first and returns on its error, then stores the pointer best-effort; loadCheckpoint repairs the ref before the pointer and ignores both repair errors. TestCheckpointPointerFailureStillPublishesReachableRef runs git gc --prune=now with a failing pointer and proves the object survives and a pointerless reader still restarts from it. That test also settles my git-gc point by execution, and components.md, limits.md and the new page all name the ref as the reachability root.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION CUTS: all taken. Sequence metadata and validateNamedSequence are hoisted out of the per-candidate loop; candidates are cheaply parsed, sorted by depth descending, then authenticated and extended only until the first success, so no duplicate materialisation and no second rev-list. Unchanged pointers are not rewritten, guarded both at the call site and inside Store, with pointer.writes asserted equal to 1. DisallowUnknownFields, the second decode and the redundant Chmod are gone, the canonical byte comparison carries the check alone. Sorting on an unauthenticated depth is safe: it only orders attempts, and a forged deeper candidate still fails authentication and falls through, which the tamper test exercises.\n\nTHE LARGER QUESTION: answered adequately, and I accept it. The head now names each selector's own job - the ref is the garbage-collection root and repairs a lost pointer, the pointer is process-independent application state that recovers from an unavailable or rewritten ref - and says plainly that neither is authority. That is an honest division of labour rather than a circular one, and it does not overclaim that the ref was incapable, which is right, because at the merge base the ref already gave every local process a warm start. I record for the file that a stronger argument exists and is not yet written down: the ref is a CAS target, so a writer that loses the race still records its deeper checkpoint in the pointer, and deepest-wins selection then keeps it. Not a change request.\n\nARCHITECTURE CONCLUSION. Layers: Ordinary Git storage (internal/gitstore) untouched. Kernel (internal/kernel) - contract changed and the change is recorded in the same head; the kernel gains an optional host-supplied opaque selector and gives it no authority, every candidate still passing the complete identity, sequence, payload, rotation and signature path. Application host and boundary adapter (internal/app) - contract changed and recorded; it owns the bounded pointer file, the off switch and invalidation. CLI and documentation surface (cmd/gs, docs) - one added command, usage string, docs/README index and five updated pages. The architecture.md update is complete and accurate for all of them.\n\nSECURITY CONCLUSION. No unresolved defect. I verified the moved parser by execution rather than by reading: a probe in a throwaway worktree confirmed fileCheckpointPointer.Load rejects a trailing newline, reordered keys, added whitespace, an unknown field, a wrong schema, an empty commit, trailing JSON, non-JSON, and a 5 KiB oversize body, and rejects a directory at the pointer path; Store replaces a symlink instead of writing through it, leaving the link target's contents intact, does not rewrite an unchanged value, and leaves mode 0600. validateObjectID still runs before the first Git command that touches a candidate. checkpoint-clear needs no actor identity, but it only slows local reads and cannot touch the durable sequence. One nit, not blocking: the off switch matches the exact string \"off\", so GITSEQ_CHECKPOINT=false or OFF silently leaves checkpoints enabled.\n\nWHAT I AM ASKING FOR, and it is small. The new page docs/reference/gs/checkpoint-clear.md rests on 835c3b31 and 6011fdfd. Both are retired - they are the artifacts of the rejected predecessor head 6451b1c1, retired when this head superseded it. Of the 49 distinct artifact citations in the whole documentation set, exactly two point at a retired artifact and both are these. After merge those two can never be superseded again, so this page can never flare however far the code moves under it, which is the one failure the discipline spends its longest passage warning about. Please cite instead the live artifacts for the behaviour the page describes, the ones main currently carries at those paths - bbe37f00 at internal/kernel and 803d9687 at internal/app read as the right pair - or whatever live pair you judge correct. Two lines of frontmatter.\n\nSTRONGLY RECOMMENDED IN THE SAME AMEND, since the head must move anyway. The bounds and canonical-JSON checks now have no regression coverage. I deleted both the 4 KiB limit and the canonical comparison from fileCheckpointPointer.Load in a throwaway worktree and internal/app, internal/kernel and cmd/gs all still passed. The kernel tests now use an in-memory fake, so the real parser is exercised only on its happy path. My probe is about twenty lines of table-driven test and would close it. Take it here or file it as a child request, your call; I am not blocking on it.\n\nNothing was merged and the reviewed branch was not edited. Local and origin tips both read 79138ad2, the worktree is clean, and all three artifacts stand at the exact head and are not stale. Everything above is verified at 79138ad2 and only at 79138ad2; the frontmatter fix moves the head, so it needs a fresh artifact and a fresh verdict, and I expect that round to be quick.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f8dac26f0f0490fe9fef4640186de52e7e3e25f1","head":"79138ad22fd88a52eb7007b5575fe996c8228472","verdict":"changes-requested"}}